H4  THE FRAMEWORK'S OWN FINDINGS AS CONSTRAINTS ON THE TARGET (source, status, what each fixes)
======================================================================================================================
    cluster-kernel share on X-COP (canonical, P2): the law removes 40% of the Newtonian dark mass when only baryons source it (FP22's adopted reading), 57% when the extra component sources it too (the record's '74-89%' reading)
    cluster-kernel share on X-COP (canonical, nu_mono): the law removes 47% of the Newtonian dark mass when only baryons source it (FP22's adopted reading), 69% when the extra component sources it too (the record's '74-89%' reading)
    cluster-kernel share on X-COP (alt, P2): the law removes 45% of the Newtonian dark mass when only baryons source it (FP22's adopted reading), 64% when the extra component sources it too (the record's '74-89%' reading)
    cluster-kernel share on X-COP (alt, nu_mono): the law removes 52% of the Newtonian dark mass when only baryons source it (FP22's adopted reading), 74% when the extra component sources it too (the record's '74-89%' reading)
    * flat a0(z)
        source: qwen_claude_field_theory/papers_2026/PAPER7_a0z_decisive_measurement_2026.tex (DOI 22833314 v3)
        status: TIED (Lambda constant)
        in the target: a0 is z-independent on a true Lambda: the law's a0 carries no z; the switch must keep the z = 2.5 discs ON (part 2)
    * a0(z) ~ sqrt(rho_DE(z)) (labelled branch)
        source: real_research/derivation_chain_2026/FP0_core_postulates.py R3b; XR20 (the sqrt V refinement)
        status: TIED (to the dark-energy fit)
